WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:03.419
With all the under the hood stuff out of the way,

2
00:00:03.419 --> 00:00:05.339
let's take a look at what the actual DNA

3
00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:06.900
of an Unreal game looks like

4
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.539
and how we can best use it to build our games.

5
00:00:09.539 --> 00:00:12.220
As mentioned previously, UObject is the base class

6
00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:13.960
for all UE4 types,

7
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.820
including all assets inside the content browser.

8
00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:18.980
They have no physical representation in the world

9
00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:21.820
and they have no built in tick functionality.

10
00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:23.679
As they've got no concept of the world,

11
00:00:23.679 --> 00:00:26.449
they have no ability to update the frame.

12
00:00:26.449 --> 00:00:27.929
They must be given an outer.

13
00:00:27.929 --> 00:00:30.730
This is the object that this object resides in.

14
00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:32.369
If one is not given an outer,

15
00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:34.289
it's put inside the transient package,

16
00:00:34.289 --> 00:00:35.609
which is the topmost package used

17
00:00:35.609 --> 00:00:39.460
for storing temporary objects, which should not be saved.

18
00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:41.100
Objects can support networking,

19
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.259
but they do not come with it set about the box.

20
00:00:43.259 --> 00:00:44.600
If networking is required,

21
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:46.460
perhaps reconsider whether or not UObject

22
00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:47.299
is the right solution

23
00:00:47.299 --> 00:00:49.719
for your current technical requirement.

24
00:00:49.719 --> 00:00:50.960
As an example of a UObject,

25
00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.200
we have the UExplosionData asset,

26
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.710
which is used to define properties regarding an explosion.

27
00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:59.270
Actor extends directly off of UObject

28
00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:01.149
and is usually accompanied by a scene component

29
00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:02.990
as its route, giving it a physical representation

30
00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:04.260
in the scene.

31
00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.500
For example, consider an exploding barrel.

32
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.819
This would be an actor consisting of smaller discrete

33
00:01:08.819 --> 00:01:11.299
pieces of functionality and a physical representation

34
00:01:11.299 --> 00:01:13.579
through some flavor of mesh.

35
00:01:13.579 --> 00:01:15.780
Actors do have networking support by default,

36
00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.500
giving us access to RPCs like we discussed earlier,

37
00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.150
as well as standard variable replication.

38
00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:22.629
They're exceptionally well suited

39
00:01:22.629 --> 00:01:24.189
to a component based architecture,

40
00:01:24.189 --> 00:01:26.909
as opposed to a giant monolithic class.

41
00:01:26.909 --> 00:01:28.870
So at this time, consider the following.

42
00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:30.629
If my current technical design requires

43
00:01:30.629 --> 00:01:32.730
either networking or ticking,

44
00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:34.370
go with actors and components.

45
00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:35.709
If you don't require either,

46
00:01:35.709 --> 00:01:38.200
consider using the UObject.

47
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.319
Actor components allow us to roll functionality

48
00:01:40.319 --> 00:01:42.079
into a single class.

49
00:01:42.079 --> 00:01:43.519
They require an actor to exist

50
00:01:43.519 --> 00:01:45.700
because they cannot simply exist in the world.

51
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.239
They're designed to be part of an actor.

52
00:01:48.239 --> 00:01:49.840
And they're excellent when used in conjunction

53
00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:51.439
with Blueprint to expose their parameters

54
00:01:51.439 --> 00:01:54.200
to design with sensible UProperty use.

55
00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:55.319
So as an example,

56
00:01:55.319 --> 00:01:56.760
we can add an explosion component

57
00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:58.719
to our exploding barrel actor

58
00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.359
and keep the actor class completely unpolluted.

59
00:02:01.359 --> 00:02:02.560
If another actor in our game

60
00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.920
requires some kind of explosion functionality,

61
00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:07.000
we can simply add the component to that class as well,

62
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.139
without having to duplicate anything.

63
00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:11.400
We can also add UProperties to the explosion component

64
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.219
to allow design to plug in an explosion data asset

65
00:02:14.219 --> 00:02:16.020
in as a field, letting them balance

66
00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:18.349
and tweak the functionality separately.

67
00:02:18.349 --> 00:02:20.270
Actor components are not particularly ideal

68
00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:21.789
for networked functionality,

69
00:02:21.789 --> 00:02:23.990
as dormancy can only be set on an actor level,

70
00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:26.090
as opposed to a component level.

71
00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:28.409
A dormant actor will send all of its components dormant

72
00:02:28.409 --> 00:02:30.370
as well, so we may end up with erroneous

73
00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:34.099
or unacceptable behavior as the project grows.

74
00:02:34.099 --> 00:02:36.979
A scene component extends off of actor component

75
00:02:36.979 --> 00:02:38.099
with the benefit of providing

76
00:02:38.099 --> 00:02:40.819
a physical representation in the scene.

77
00:02:40.819 --> 00:02:42.539
They can be attached to other scene components

78
00:02:42.539 --> 00:02:44.580
as well as bones and sockets.

79
00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:45.620
And they can also be used

80
00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:47.819
to detect collisions and overlaps.

81
00:02:47.819 --> 00:02:49.900
It's the scene component that gives a base actor

82
00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.259
its physical representation in the scene.

83
00:02:52.259 --> 00:02:54.620
An actor itself has got no intrinsic transform,

84
00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:56.659
but it does have a root component,

85
00:02:56.699 --> 00:02:58.139
just simple scene component

86
00:02:58.139 --> 00:03:00.300
giving us our scene representation.
